-      ],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
-      "passes": true
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "US-928",
-      "title": "OWASP LLM01: Prompt injection input sanitisation and output fencing",
-      "priority": "high",
-      "description": "OWASP LLM Top 10 (2025) identifies Prompt Injection as the top security risk for LLM applications. A malicious WhatsApp or webchat user could craft messages that override the AI's system prompt, exfiltrate knowledge base content, or trigger unintended tool calls. Rainbow AI currently has no documented input sanitisation layer or output fencing between the AI response and tool execution.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "Inbound WhatsApp/webchat messages are scanned for known prompt injection patterns before being passed to the LLM (e.g., 'ignore previous instructions', role-play prefixes, base64-encoded instructions)",
-        "System prompt content is never reflected verbatim in AI responses â€” a post-processing check redacts content between <system> tags if present in output",
-        "Tool call arguments produced by the LLM are validated against a JSON schema before execution â€” no arbitrary SQL, shell commands, or URLs are accepted as tool inputs",
-        "Injection attempts are logged with the offending message content (redacted PII) and a security alert is emitted",
-        "Vitest unit tests cover: clean message passes, known injection pattern is flagged, tool argument schema violation is rejected"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "Reference: OWASP LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection at genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/",
-        "Pattern library: common injection strings (jailbreaks, DAN prompts, instruction override phrases)",
-        "Output fencing: post-process LLM response to strip <system>...</system> and [INST]...[/INST] blocks",
-        "Tool call validation: Zod schema parse on tool arguments before dispatch",
-        "Low false-positive risk: check patterns against message context (guest asking about rooms vs injection attempt)"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
-      "passes": true
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "US-929",
-      "title": "OWASP LLM08: Excessive agency guardrails â€” restrict AI tool call permissions by intent",
-      "priority": "high",
-      "description": "OWASP LLM08 Excessive Agency identifies that LLMs granted broad tool access may take unintended high-impact actions. Rainbow AI's tool-call loop has access to booking, messaging, order submission, and housekeeping tools. Without intent-scoped tool permissions, a misclassified intent or adversarial prompt could trigger destructive actions. A permission matrix tying tool access to verified intent classes limits blast radius.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "A tool permission matrix is defined in config mapping each intent class to a whitelist of permitted tool names",
-        "The tool-call loop enforces the whitelist: any tool not in the permitted set for the active intent is rejected with a log entry",
-        "Destructive tools (cancel_booking, send_template_message, submit_order) require a minimum confidence threshold (>=0.85) before execution",
-        "If the AI attempts a disallowed tool call, the loop returns a safe fallback response rather than erroring",
-        "Vitest tests cover: allowed tool is called, disallowed tool is blocked, low-confidence destructive call is deferred to human"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "Reference: OWASP LLM08:2025 Excessive Agency at genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/",
-        "Config location: src/assistant/data/tool-permissions.json or extend routing.json",
-        "Confidence threshold enforcement: check intent.confidence before dispatching destructive tools",
-        "Audit log: record every tool dispatch with intent, tool name, confidence, and outcome"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [
-        "Tiered confidence-based fallback with progressive escalation"
