-    {
-      "id": "US-937",
-      "title": "AI waiter: multi-language menu item name lookup for Chinese and Malay terms",
-      "priority": "medium",
-      "description": "Makan Moments Cafe serves a multilingual Malaysian customer base. Guests ordering in Mandarin or Malay may use localised item names that do not match the English menu item names stored in the system (e.g., 'ç‚’é¥­' for fried rice, 'nasi goreng' for fried rice). The AI waiter's menu item disambiguation logic only matches against English names, causing unresolved items and fallback responses for non-English orders.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "Each menu item record supports an optional translations object with keys for 'ms' (Malay) and 'zh' (Simplified Chinese)",
-        "The menu item disambiguation tool searches across all localised name variants, not just English",
-        "A guest ordering 'nasi goreng' resolves to the same item as 'fried rice' in the cart",
-        "A guest ordering 'ç‚’é¥­' resolves to the same item when zh translation is configured",
-        "Admin panel menu sync API includes a translations field in the menu item schema",
-        "Vitest test: order in Malay resolves correct item; order in Chinese resolves correct item; unrecognised term returns disambiguation prompt"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "Extend menu item schema: add translations: Record<string, string> field",
-        "Disambiguation lookup: search translations values in addition to item.name",
-        "Fuzzy match: apply existing fuzzy matching (Levenshtein/trigram) across all locale variants",
-        "Complements: Menu item disambiguation story (English) and Automatic language detection story"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [
-        "Menu item disambiguation: handle partial or ambiguous item names in orders",
-        "Automatic language detection and response in Chinese and Tamil"
-      ],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
-      "passes": false
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "US-938",
-      "title": "LLM reliability: exponential backoff with jitter on AI provider timeouts",
-      "priority": "medium",
-      "description": "Google's 5 Pillars of LLM Reliability recommends exponential backoff with jitter for transient AI provider failures to avoid thundering herd. Rainbow AI's current provider failover switches to the next provider immediately on error, but does not retry the same provider with backoff for transient timeout errors. This causes unnecessary provider switches for brief network blips, increasing cost and latency variance.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "Provider timeout errors (HTTP 429, 503, or network timeout) trigger a retry on the same provider with exponential backoff: 1s, 2s, 4s (max 3 retries)",
-        "Each retry delay includes plus/minus 20% random jitter to avoid synchronised retries from concurrent conversations",
-        "After max retries are exhausted, failover to the next provider in the chain is triggered",
-        "HTTP 400/401/422 (permanent errors) do not trigger retry â€” they immediately fail over",
-        "Retry attempt count and total latency are logged per conversation for observability",
-        "Vitest test: 429 response triggers 3 retries with backoff before failing over; 401 immediately fails over"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "Reference: Google Cloud blog 'The Five Pillars of LLM Reliability' â€” Pillar 2: Retry Logic",
-        "Implementation: src/lib/failover.ts â€” add retry wrapper around provider call with backoff calculator",
-        "Backoff formula: delay = Math.min(baseDelay * 2^attempt, maxDelay) * (1 + jitter * (Math.random() - 0.5) * 2)",
-        "Complements existing story: Baileys circuit breaker â€” apply same pattern to AI providers"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [
-        "chatWithToolsLoop graceful fallback: answer without tools if all tool calls fail"
-      ],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
-      "passes": true
-    },
-    {
