-      "id": "US-960",
-      "title": "Support BSUID as primary contact identifier in webhook handling",
-      "priority": "critical",
-      "description": "From June 2026, WhatsApp is rolling out usernames that allow users to hide phone numbers. When a user adopts a username, their phone number may no longer appear in webhook payloads â€” instead a Business-Scoped User ID (BSUID, up to 128 alphanumeric chars, prefixed `whatsapp:CC.BSUID`) is delivered in the `from`, `to`, and new `ExternalUserId` fields. All conversation-tracking, CRM mapping, and message dispatch logic that assumes `from`/`to` are always E.164 phone numbers will break for those users.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "Webhook handler accepts and stores BSUID from `ExternalUserId` field on every inbound message, regardless of whether the user has a username",
-        "Conversation-lookup logic falls back gracefully when `from` is a BSUID rather than a phone number",
-        "Outbound messaging supports BSUID as the `to` address when a phone number is unavailable",
-        "Existing conversations previously keyed on phone number are migrated/linked to the corresponding BSUID in the database",
-        "BSUID â†” phone-number mapping is preserved for the rolling 30-day window where both are returned",
-        "Authentication templates (one-tap, zero-tap, copy-code) continue to use phone number as they cannot use BSUID"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "BSUID format: `whatsapp:CC.BSUID`, max 140 chars including prefix",
-        "Both phone number and BSUID are returned for 30 days after any interaction; BSUID is always present in `ExternalUserId`",
-        "Contact Book feature launches April 2026 and automatically stores phoneâ†”BSUID pairs for known contacts",
-        "Reference: Twilio changelog â€” https://www.twilio.com/en-us/changelog/whatsapp-usernames--new-business-scoped-user-id--bsuid--field-re"
-      ],
