-    {
-      "id": "US-971",
-      "title": "Implement PDPA 2024 automated-decision transparency disclosure for AI responses",
-      "priority": "medium",
-      "description": "Malaysia's National Guidelines on AI Governance and Ethics (AIGE, September 2024) and the PDPA 2024 transparency principles require that when AI systems make decisions or recommendations affecting individuals, businesses must disclose that AI is being used, what data informs the decision, and provide a mechanism for human review or appeal. While AIGE compliance is currently voluntary, it aligns with PDPA transparency obligations that are legally binding, and hostel guests must be informed when automated AI determines booking advice, pricing, or room availability.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "Every outbound AI-generated message includes a configurable disclosure footer (e.g., 'This response was generated by Rainbow AI') that can be toggled per-property",
-        "When the AI provides a recommendation based on personal data (e.g., room suggestions based on previous stay), the disclosure notes which data category was used",
-        "Guests can type a designated keyword (e.g., 'SPEAK HUMAN') to request escalation to a human agent at any point in the conversation",
-        "The admin dashboard shows each automated decision event with the intent classification, AI provider used, and data categories accessed",
-        "Privacy notice (accessible via 'PRIVACY' keyword) explains that responses are AI-generated and that human review is available",
-        "Audit log records the AI provider, model version, and intent classification for every outbound message for a minimum of 1 year"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "Malaysia AIGE (September 2024) â€” transparency principle requires disclosure of AI use, training data categories, and decision criteria",
-        "PDPA 2024 transparency obligations are legally binding; AIGE guidelines are voluntary but recommended",
-        "Human escalation path is also required under WhatsApp's January 2026 task-specific chatbot policy",
-        "Reference: https://fpf.org/blog/malaysia-charts-its-digital-course-a-guide-to-the-new-frameworks-for-data-protection-and-ai-ethics/"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
-      "passes": false
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "US-972",
-      "title": "Implement one-tap authentication template with App Signing Key Hash handshake",
-      "priority": "medium",
-      "description": "WhatsApp supports one-tap and zero-tap authentication templates that allow Android users to receive OTPs without leaving the app. These require a handshake between the business app and WhatsApp using the App Signing Key Hash, and the handshake must complete within 10 minutes of the template send. The current webchat authentication flow uses a plain copy-code template; upgrading to one-tap would reduce friction for Android users (who are the majority in Malaysia) and improve OTP conversion rates.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "An authentication template is created with a one-tap autofill button including the app's package name and signing key hash in the `components` array",
-        "The mobile app initiates the handshake with WhatsApp before requesting the OTP template send",
-        "The template send is made within 10 minutes of handshake initiation; a timeout guard retries the handshake if the window expires",
-        "On non-Android devices (iOS, desktop), the template automatically falls back to a copy-code button without error",
-        "Zero-tap variant is available as an optional configuration for fully automated OTP capture; it requires the Android broadcast receiver integration",
-        "Template is categorized as `AUTHENTICATION` and charged per message (not as marketing)"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "One-tap requires: package name, app signing key hash in template components; handshake via `GET /v1/whatsapp/handshake`",
-        "Handshake window: 10 minutes; if it expires before template send, the autofill button will degrade to copy-code",
-        "Zero-tap: Android-only; uses BroadcastReceiver to auto-capture the OTP; also requires App Signing Key Hash",
-        "Authentication templates cannot use BSUID as recipient â€” must use E.164 phone number",
-        "Reference: https://developer.8x8.com/connect/docs/guide-whatsapp-one-tap-or-zero-tap-authentication/"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "medium",
-      "passes": false
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "US-973",
-      "title": "Implement utility template reclassification guard to prevent marketing mischarge",
-      "priority": "medium",
-      "description": "Under WhatsApp's July 2025 per-message pricing rules, utility templates that include any promotional content (upsells, cross-sells, or content that appears 'even slightly promotional') are automatically reclassified as marketing templates by Meta and charged at the higher marketing rate. This reclassification is applied post-send by Meta, not at approval time, and can cause unexpected cost spikes. The current AI-generated order confirmation and booking confirmation messages may include upsell language that triggers reclassification.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "A pre-send lint check scans outbound utility templates for promotional language patterns (discount words, offer language, upsell phrases) and blocks send with a warning if detected",
-        "Operator UI shows a 'Utility Template Risk' badge when a template draft contains any of the flagged promotional patterns",
-        "Webhook handler for message status events detects when a message sent as utility is confirmed delivered as marketing (Meta returns a category in delivery receipts) and logs a reclassification event",
-        "Admin dashboard includes a 'Reclassification Events' report showing affected messages, original category, reclassified category, and cost delta",
-        "Monthly cost reconciliation report flags any month where reclassification events account for >5% of utility message spend"
